1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:03,240
Hello everyone, and welcome to Two Cups English.

2
00:00:03,840 --> 00:00:06,699
I'm Mia, and Ben is here with me.

3
00:00:06,780 --> 00:00:09,580
This podcast is for people who are learning English.

4
00:00:10,220 --> 00:00:11,080
We speak slowly.

5
00:00:11,620 --> 00:00:12,980
We use simple words.

6
00:00:13,560 --> 00:00:16,419
And we repeat the important words many times.

7
00:00:17,300 --> 00:00:20,260
Today, our topic is the perfect moment.

8
00:00:20,900 --> 00:00:22,940
The moment when everything is ready.

9
00:00:23,920 --> 00:00:26,020
Ben, are you waiting for something right now?

10
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:27,480
Hi Mia.

11
00:00:27,920 --> 00:00:28,440
Hi everyone.

12
00:00:29,230 --> 00:00:31,740
Yes, I am always waiting for something.

13
00:00:32,580 --> 00:00:34,740
Right now, I am waiting to fix my bike.

14
00:00:36,080 --> 00:00:41,580
I said, "I will fix it when I have a free Saturday." That was in March.

15
00:00:42,400 --> 00:00:44,000
It is now September.

16
00:00:45,460 --> 00:00:47,200
The bike is still broken.

17
00:00:48,320 --> 00:00:49,060
What about you, Mia?

18
00:00:49,670 --> 00:00:50,720
Are you waiting for something?

19
00:00:51,960 --> 00:00:52,760
I am, Ben.

20
00:00:53,340 --> 00:00:55,760
For two years, I wanted to learn to swim.

21
00:00:56,340 --> 00:00:57,890
I was thirty-one.

22
00:00:57,940 --> 00:01:07,920
I said, "I will start when I have time." Then, "I will start in summer." Then, "when work is calm." Two years passed.

23
00:01:08,320 --> 00:01:09,260
I did not swim.

24
00:01:09,940 --> 00:01:10,480
I waited.

25
00:01:11,260 --> 00:01:13,380
And the perfect moment never came.

26
00:01:14,120 --> 00:01:16,520
Not one perfect day in two years.

27
00:01:17,480 --> 00:01:18,890
I think many people know this.

28
00:01:19,470 --> 00:01:21,040
We wait for the perfect moment.

29
00:01:21,660 --> 00:01:24,800
And the perfect moment is not coming.

30
00:01:24,910 --> 00:01:29,620
"The perfect moment is not coming." That is our topic today, Mia.

31
00:01:30,400 --> 00:01:32,700
And I think it is good news, actually.

32
00:01:33,660 --> 00:01:34,880
So here is our plan.

33
00:01:35,720 --> 00:01:38,970
In part one, we tell our own waiting stories.

34
00:01:39,900 --> 00:01:42,160
Mia's swimming, and my podcast.

35
00:01:43,240 --> 00:01:47,520
In part two, we ask why the perfect moment never comes.

36
00:01:48,400 --> 00:01:50,610
And I have a true story for that.

37
00:01:51,660 --> 00:01:55,440
In part three, we share simple ideas for starting now.

38
00:01:56,540 --> 00:01:57,260
Does that sound good?

39
00:01:58,160 --> 00:01:59,200
That sounds good, Ben.

40
00:01:59,540 --> 00:02:03,760
And to everyone listening, please do not worry if you miss something.

41
00:02:04,260 --> 00:02:05,200
You will hear it again.

42
00:02:05,940 --> 00:02:09,959
You can listen while you walk, while you cook, or while you wait for the bus.

43
00:02:10,960 --> 00:02:11,560
Okay, Ben.

44
00:02:11,800 --> 00:02:12,360
Part one.

45
00:02:12,700 --> 00:02:13,280
My swimming.

46
00:02:13,960 --> 00:02:15,280
Do you want to know how it ended?

47
00:02:16,280 --> 00:02:17,180
Of course, Mia.

48
00:02:17,820 --> 00:02:20,140
I have wanted to ask you this for weeks.

49
00:02:20,980 --> 00:02:21,980
Did you swim in the end?

50
00:02:22,640 --> 00:02:24,720
Or is the pool still waiting for you?

51
00:02:25,700 --> 00:02:26,480
I did, Ben.

52
00:02:26,700 --> 00:02:28,780
And it was not a perfect moment.

53
00:02:29,360 --> 00:02:30,200
It was a Tuesday.

54
00:02:30,700 --> 00:02:31,400
It was raining.

55
00:02:31,880 --> 00:02:32,580
I was tired.

56
00:02:33,200 --> 00:02:34,340
I had a bad day at work.

57
00:02:34,840 --> 00:02:37,020
And on the way home, I passed the pool.

58
00:02:37,920 --> 00:02:40,460
And I thought, "This is a bad moment.

59
00:02:41,020 --> 00:02:41,740
I am tired.

60
00:02:42,040 --> 00:02:42,740
I am sad.

61
00:02:43,180 --> 00:02:43,960
It is raining."

62
00:02:45,140 --> 00:02:46,460
A bad moment, Mia.

63
00:02:47,260 --> 00:02:49,460
Tired, sad, and raining.

64
00:02:50,360 --> 00:02:52,300
That is three reasons to go home.

65
00:02:53,320 --> 00:02:54,360
So what happened?

66
00:02:55,160 --> 00:02:55,810
Did you go home?

67
00:02:56,920 --> 00:03:02,320
Then I thought, "Every moment was a bad moment for two years." So I went in.

68
00:03:03,020 --> 00:03:03,760
I bought a ticket.

69
00:03:04,400 --> 00:03:07,180
I swam for ten minutes, badly.

70
00:03:07,300 --> 00:03:08,220
And I started.

71
00:03:08,880 --> 00:03:09,960
On a bad Tuesday.

72
00:03:10,580 --> 00:03:11,520
Not a perfect day.

73
00:03:12,000 --> 00:03:12,690
Not a Monday.

74
00:03:13,140 --> 00:03:13,880
Not a new year.

75
00:03:14,580 --> 00:03:16,200
A bad, rainy Tuesday.

76
00:03:17,020 --> 00:03:19,730
"On a bad Tuesday." I love that, Mia.

77
00:03:20,280 --> 00:03:22,140
Because we think starting needs a good day.

78
00:03:22,720 --> 00:03:23,210
A Monday.

79
00:03:23,760 --> 00:03:24,840
A new year.

80
00:03:24,900 --> 00:03:26,800
A sunny morning with energy.

81
00:03:26,820 --> 00:03:28,920
But most starts happen on bad Tuesdays.

82
00:03:29,780 --> 00:03:31,100
Let me tell you my story now.

83
00:03:32,060 --> 00:03:34,360
For three years, I wanted to make this podcast.

84
00:03:34,960 --> 00:03:35,980
Three years, Ben?

85
00:03:36,500 --> 00:03:37,680
Longer than my swimming.

86
00:03:37,980 --> 00:03:39,040
What were you waiting for?

87
00:03:39,440 --> 00:03:40,320
A better voice?

88
00:03:40,660 --> 00:03:41,500
A better plan?

89
00:03:42,360 --> 00:03:43,720
I was waiting to be ready, Mia.

90
00:03:44,420 --> 00:03:45,260
I had the idea.

91
00:03:45,760 --> 00:03:46,460
I had the name.

92
00:03:47,040 --> 00:03:48,120
I had a list of topics.

93
00:03:48,630 --> 00:03:59,689
And I said, "I need a better microphone." Then, "I need to speak better first." Then, "I need to be ready." Three years of "I need to be ready."

94
00:04:00,380 --> 00:04:04,420
"I need to be ready." Ben, that is the sentence, isn't it?

95
00:04:04,500 --> 00:04:05,820
Everyone says it.

96
00:04:05,880 --> 00:04:06,840
What does ready mean?

97
00:04:07,800 --> 00:04:08,720
I did not know, Mia.

98
00:04:09,280 --> 00:04:10,120
That was the problem.

99
00:04:10,900 --> 00:04:13,320
Ready was a feeling I was waiting for.

100
00:04:13,380 --> 00:04:15,240
A feeling of, "Now.

101
00:04:15,320 --> 00:04:18,080
Now I can do it." And the feeling never came.

102
00:04:18,860 --> 00:04:21,680
Because ready is not a feeling that comes before.

103
00:04:21,740 --> 00:04:24,020
Ready is a feeling that comes after.

104
00:04:24,140 --> 00:04:24,860
After the start.

105
00:04:25,640 --> 00:04:27,020
After the first bad episode.

106
00:04:27,700 --> 00:04:29,340
After your first ten minutes in the pool.

107
00:04:30,170 --> 00:04:34,320
"Ready comes after the start." Ben, I want to write that down.

108
00:04:34,380 --> 00:04:36,290
Nobody feels ready before.

109
00:04:36,360 --> 00:04:37,840
Everyone feels ready after.

110
00:04:38,580 --> 00:04:40,640
So how did you start the podcast, in the end?

111
00:04:42,160 --> 00:04:43,420
My sister, Mia.

112
00:04:43,480 --> 00:04:45,280
She was tired of hearing about it.

113
00:04:45,380 --> 00:04:52,580
One evening, she said, "Ben, record one episode tonight, with your phone, or stop talking about it." I was angry.

114
00:04:53,280 --> 00:04:55,380
Then I recorded one episode with my phone.

115
00:04:56,140 --> 00:04:57,400
With your phone!

116
00:04:58,020 --> 00:04:59,140
In your kitchen, I guess.

117
00:04:59,720 --> 00:05:00,260
Was it good?

118
00:05:00,740 --> 00:05:01,640
Be honest, Ben.

119
00:05:02,520 --> 00:05:04,120
It was terrible, Mia.

120
00:05:04,180 --> 00:05:05,160
My voice was shaking.

121
00:05:05,760 --> 00:05:07,340
I said "um" fifty times.

122
00:05:07,920 --> 00:05:09,220
But it existed.

123
00:05:09,280 --> 00:05:10,600
It was real.

124
00:05:10,610 --> 00:05:13,500
And the next day, I recorded another one.

125
00:05:13,560 --> 00:05:15,480
A little less terrible.

126
00:05:15,520 --> 00:05:17,580
The perfect moment did not come.

127
00:05:17,640 --> 00:05:18,940
My sister came.

128
00:05:18,970 --> 00:05:20,160
And a phone.

129
00:05:20,220 --> 00:05:20,820
That was enough.

130
00:05:21,820 --> 00:05:23,300
A sister and a phone.

131
00:05:23,360 --> 00:05:25,220
That is a perfect moment, actually, Ben.

132
00:05:25,880 --> 00:05:27,860
Just not the kind we imagine.

133
00:05:27,899 --> 00:05:29,360
We imagine a big beginning.

134
00:05:29,760 --> 00:05:30,350
A big day.

135
00:05:30,980 --> 00:05:33,070
But the real beginning is a phone in the kitchen.

136
00:05:33,660 --> 00:05:35,620
Ten minutes in a pool.

137
00:05:35,680 --> 00:05:37,260
Did you post that first episode?

138
00:05:38,500 --> 00:05:39,580
I wanted to delete it, Mia.

139
00:05:40,320 --> 00:05:41,640
It was not ready for the world.

140
00:05:42,409 --> 00:05:45,200
My sister said, "Post it." I posted it.

141
00:05:45,940 --> 00:05:46,870
Nine people listened.

142
00:05:47,420 --> 00:05:50,969
One wrote, "Thank you, this helped me." One person.

143
00:05:51,920 --> 00:05:54,870
And that one person was worth more than three years of waiting.

144
00:05:55,720 --> 00:05:57,790
In three years of waiting, I helped nobody.

145
00:05:58,600 --> 00:06:01,220
In one bad episode, I helped one person.

146
00:06:02,189 --> 00:06:07,040
"In three years of waiting, I helped nobody." Ben, that is the price of waiting.

147
00:06:07,580 --> 00:06:09,500
And we do not see the price until later.

148
00:06:10,260 --> 00:06:11,920
Can I tell you about my "later" list?

149
00:06:12,880 --> 00:06:13,620
A "later" list?

150
00:06:14,160 --> 00:06:15,440
I think I have one too, Mia.

151
00:06:15,980 --> 00:06:17,040
But I never wrote it down.

152
00:06:17,440 --> 00:06:18,450
Please, tell us.

153
00:06:19,200 --> 00:06:21,760
I had one, on paper, Ben.

154
00:06:22,600 --> 00:06:23,980
Learn to swim, later.

155
00:06:24,660 --> 00:06:26,940
Call my old friend, later.

156
00:06:26,980 --> 00:06:29,120
Fix the shelf, later.

157
00:06:29,140 --> 00:06:31,080
Write to my grandmother, later.

158
00:06:32,860 --> 00:06:34,360
My grandmother died last year.

159
00:06:35,190 --> 00:06:36,820
The letter was still on the list.

160
00:06:38,220 --> 00:06:39,140
I am sorry, Mia.

161
00:06:40,150 --> 00:06:41,380
And thank you for telling us that.

162
00:06:42,380 --> 00:06:44,400
Because that is the real reason for today.

163
00:06:45,260 --> 00:06:45,980
Not success.

164
00:06:46,840 --> 00:06:47,360
Not work.

165
00:06:48,350 --> 00:06:53,520
"Later" has a price, and the price is the things we never do.

166
00:06:53,600 --> 00:06:54,459
Yes, Ben.

167
00:06:54,600 --> 00:06:56,520
I do not say it to make everyone sad.

168
00:06:57,140 --> 00:07:00,280
I say it because "later" is not a real place.

169
00:07:00,920 --> 00:07:03,300
Later is where all the perfect moments live.

170
00:07:03,820 --> 00:07:05,160
And nobody lives there.

171
00:07:06,760 --> 00:07:08,440
Okay, so that was part one.

172
00:07:09,040 --> 00:07:17,060
Two years of swimming, three years of a podcast, a bad Tuesday, a sister with a phone, and a letter that stayed on a list.

173
00:07:17,940 --> 00:07:19,260
Now, part two.

174
00:07:20,380 --> 00:07:22,580
Why does the perfect moment never come?

175
00:07:23,460 --> 00:07:24,980
Ben, you have a true story.

176
00:07:26,020 --> 00:07:27,560
I do, Mia.

177
00:07:27,640 --> 00:07:29,580
Okay, part two.

178
00:07:29,640 --> 00:07:30,740
This is a true story.

179
00:07:31,380 --> 00:07:37,120
In January 2007, Steve Jobs stood on a stage and showed the first iPhone to the world.

180
00:07:38,020 --> 00:07:39,099
Everyone remembers that.

181
00:07:39,760 --> 00:07:40,560
It looked perfect.

182
00:07:41,550 --> 00:07:48,340
But a writer named Fred Vogelstein later told the story behind it, in The New York Times Magazine, in 2013.

183
00:07:49,220 --> 00:07:52,040
And the iPhone on that stage was not finished.

184
00:07:52,860 --> 00:07:53,760
Not finished?

185
00:07:54,110 --> 00:07:55,640
The first iPhone, Ben?

186
00:07:56,080 --> 00:07:57,560
The one everyone remembers?

187
00:07:58,180 --> 00:07:59,940
I thought that phone was perfect.

188
00:08:00,940 --> 00:08:02,040
Not finished, Mia.

189
00:08:02,680 --> 00:08:04,860
The phone on the stage had many problems.

190
00:08:05,500 --> 00:08:06,260
It could crash.

191
00:08:06,900 --> 00:08:09,260
The engineers had a special order for the demo.

192
00:08:09,960 --> 00:08:12,180
Do this, then this, then this.

193
00:08:13,120 --> 00:08:17,800
If Jobs did something in a different order, the phone could stop working.

194
00:08:17,920 --> 00:08:19,850
Some parts of the software were not done.

195
00:08:20,980 --> 00:08:22,520
So the team was afraid, Ben.

196
00:08:22,980 --> 00:08:24,840
And Jobs went on the stage anyway?

197
00:08:25,980 --> 00:08:27,080
He went anyway, Mia.

198
00:08:27,840 --> 00:08:29,620
He did the demo in the right order.

199
00:08:30,440 --> 00:08:30,930
It worked.

200
00:08:31,740 --> 00:08:34,780
And six months later, the finished phone went to the shops.

201
00:08:35,820 --> 00:08:39,720
So the most famous product of our time started before it was ready.

202
00:08:40,690 --> 00:08:44,420
"Before it was ready." Ben, I think about this a lot.

203
00:08:45,000 --> 00:08:49,040
If Apple waited for the perfect iPhone, we might still be waiting.

204
00:08:49,760 --> 00:08:51,380
Every product has problems.

205
00:08:51,860 --> 00:08:56,480
Every first episode has "um." Every first swim is bad.

206
00:08:57,400 --> 00:08:58,669
So what was the difference?

207
00:08:59,140 --> 00:09:01,780
Why could Jobs start, and I could not?

208
00:09:02,840 --> 00:09:04,620
He had a plan for the problems, Mia.

209
00:09:05,400 --> 00:09:06,440
Not a perfect phone.

210
00:09:07,120 --> 00:09:08,960
A plan for an imperfect phone.

211
00:09:09,949 --> 00:09:11,000
The order for the demo.

212
00:09:11,650 --> 00:09:12,600
That was his plan.

213
00:09:13,500 --> 00:09:16,100
The people who start are not the people who are ready.

214
00:09:17,040 --> 00:09:20,590
They are the people who start anyway, with a plan for the problems.

215
00:09:21,380 --> 00:09:23,920
A plan for an imperfect phone.

216
00:09:24,000 --> 00:09:24,780
I like that, Ben.

217
00:09:25,740 --> 00:09:27,560
Let me say one more thing about waiting.

218
00:09:28,320 --> 00:09:30,500
Waiting feels like something.

219
00:09:30,560 --> 00:09:31,970
It feels like preparing.

220
00:09:32,570 --> 00:09:35,920
When I waited two years, I did not feel lazy.

221
00:09:36,020 --> 00:09:37,199
I read about swimming.

222
00:09:37,280 --> 00:09:38,560
I bought a swimsuit.

223
00:09:38,700 --> 00:09:40,120
I looked at pools on the map.

224
00:09:41,020 --> 00:09:42,740
Two years of "preparing."

225
00:09:43,780 --> 00:09:45,000
Reading about swimming, Mia.

226
00:09:45,720 --> 00:09:47,120
Buying a swimsuit.

227
00:09:47,200 --> 00:09:49,100
Looking at pools.

228
00:09:49,140 --> 00:09:50,380
It looks like work, doesn't it?

229
00:09:51,290 --> 00:09:52,680
I did the same.

230
00:09:52,740 --> 00:09:54,130
Videos about microphones.

231
00:09:54,560 --> 00:09:56,380
Articles about speaking.

232
00:09:56,540 --> 00:09:58,580
I was "getting ready" every evening.

233
00:09:59,460 --> 00:10:00,970
And I was not getting ready.

234
00:10:01,689 --> 00:10:02,480
I was waiting.

235
00:10:03,360 --> 00:10:04,189
Did you feel that too?

236
00:10:05,800 --> 00:10:07,060
I felt careful, Ben.

237
00:10:07,540 --> 00:10:08,900
Not lazy.

238
00:10:08,980 --> 00:10:09,470
Careful.

239
00:10:10,360 --> 00:10:11,860
That is the trick of waiting.

240
00:10:12,380 --> 00:10:14,360
It never feels like waiting.

241
00:10:14,380 --> 00:10:16,560
It feels like being smart.

242
00:10:16,590 --> 00:10:21,440
"I am not ready yet, so I prepare." But the pool does not care how much I read.

243
00:10:21,940 --> 00:10:24,980
The pool only knows if I am in the water or not.

244
00:10:27,000 --> 00:10:28,500
Two years of preparing, Mia.

245
00:10:29,260 --> 00:10:29,870
I know it well.

246
00:10:30,800 --> 00:10:32,860
Three years of reading about microphones.

247
00:10:33,880 --> 00:10:37,380
But waiting looks like work, and it is not work.

248
00:10:38,260 --> 00:10:42,600
Ten minutes in the water taught you more than two years of reading, right?

249
00:10:42,680 --> 00:10:43,720
Much more, Ben.

250
00:10:44,260 --> 00:10:45,810
Because the water is the teacher.

251
00:10:46,280 --> 00:10:47,400
Not the book.

252
00:10:47,460 --> 00:10:49,160
The pool is the teacher.

253
00:10:49,200 --> 00:10:50,780
The microphone is the teacher.

254
00:10:51,260 --> 00:10:53,140
The thing itself is the teacher.

255
00:10:53,760 --> 00:10:56,060
And the thing can only teach when we start.

256
00:10:57,090 --> 00:11:01,600
"The thing itself is the teacher." Mia, this is true for English, too.

257
00:11:02,440 --> 00:11:04,560
Many of our listeners are waiting.

258
00:11:04,610 --> 00:11:12,760
They say, "I will speak English when my grammar is better." "I will talk to people when I know more words." I understand.

259
00:11:13,440 --> 00:11:14,740
I said the same about Spanish.

260
00:11:15,780 --> 00:11:17,900
And how did you start speaking Spanish, Ben?

261
00:11:18,600 --> 00:11:19,880
Was there a perfect moment?

262
00:11:20,350 --> 00:11:23,240
A class, a teacher, a good day?

263
00:11:24,220 --> 00:11:26,180
At a bus stop, Mia.

264
00:11:26,300 --> 00:11:29,700
One day, I said hello to a man from Mexico at the bus stop.

265
00:11:30,499 --> 00:11:31,860
My Spanish was terrible.

266
00:11:32,680 --> 00:11:33,260
He smiled.

267
00:11:33,920 --> 00:11:35,080
We talked for two minutes.

268
00:11:35,920 --> 00:11:36,820
That was my start.

269
00:11:37,780 --> 00:11:39,100
Two minutes at a bus stop.

270
00:11:40,080 --> 00:11:41,480
Two minutes at a bus stop.

271
00:11:42,020 --> 00:11:43,620
That is a start, Ben.

272
00:11:43,660 --> 00:11:45,420
And I think this is important.

273
00:11:45,450 --> 00:11:47,200
The start is small.

274
00:11:47,240 --> 00:11:49,380
The perfect moment we wait for is big.

275
00:11:49,870 --> 00:11:54,890
But the real start is two minutes, or ten minutes, or one bad episode.

276
00:11:55,540 --> 00:11:58,640
If the start is small, we do not need a perfect moment.

277
00:11:59,040 --> 00:12:00,220
We need any moment.

278
00:12:00,630 --> 00:12:01,200
This one.

279
00:12:01,720 --> 00:12:02,140
Now.

280
00:12:03,200 --> 00:12:03,740
"This one.

281
00:12:04,580 --> 00:12:07,720
Now." Mia, that reminds me of a small thing.

282
00:12:08,660 --> 00:12:10,600
I wanted to call my father every Sunday.

283
00:12:11,400 --> 00:12:14,840
And every Sunday, I waited for the right time.

284
00:12:14,940 --> 00:12:16,200
After lunch?

285
00:12:16,240 --> 00:12:17,960
No, he sleeps.

286
00:12:18,080 --> 00:12:18,380
Evening?

287
00:12:19,160 --> 00:12:19,990
No, the football.

288
00:12:21,060 --> 00:12:21,420
Morning?

289
00:12:22,100 --> 00:12:22,620
Too early.

290
00:12:23,700 --> 00:12:24,780
And the Sunday passed.

291
00:12:25,580 --> 00:12:27,100
Every Sunday.

292
00:12:27,140 --> 00:12:27,750
For months.

293
00:12:28,860 --> 00:12:31,540
Waiting for the right hour to make a five-minute call.

294
00:12:32,700 --> 00:12:33,700
Five minutes, Ben.

295
00:12:34,400 --> 00:12:36,580
And months of waiting for the right hour.

296
00:12:37,280 --> 00:12:37,970
What do you do now?

297
00:12:38,860 --> 00:12:40,260
I call when I think of it, Mia.

298
00:12:40,790 --> 00:12:42,560
That is the rule now.

299
00:12:42,600 --> 00:12:46,820
When the thought comes, "I should call Dad," I call.

300
00:12:46,880 --> 00:12:47,410
Not later.

301
00:12:48,340 --> 00:12:48,630
Then.

302
00:12:49,580 --> 00:12:52,060
Sometimes he is sleeping, and he does not answer.

303
00:12:52,940 --> 00:12:53,240
Fine.

304
00:12:53,840 --> 00:12:54,560
I call again.

305
00:12:55,380 --> 00:12:57,080
The thought is the moment.

306
00:12:58,040 --> 00:13:00,380
And that is the word I want to talk about.

307
00:13:01,370 --> 00:13:05,620
"Now." Now is the only moment we have.

308
00:13:05,660 --> 00:13:08,100
But now always feels wrong for starting.

309
00:13:09,060 --> 00:13:11,040
So we push the start to "later."

310
00:13:11,840 --> 00:13:13,660
And later does not exist, Ben.

311
00:13:14,060 --> 00:13:14,780
We said that.

312
00:13:15,820 --> 00:13:16,880
Can I ask about fear?

313
00:13:17,360 --> 00:13:21,460
Because I think fear is what we are really talking about.

314
00:13:21,480 --> 00:13:23,440
You are right, Mia.

315
00:13:23,500 --> 00:13:26,080
When I waited for the perfect moment, I was afraid.

316
00:13:26,860 --> 00:13:28,840
Afraid of being bad.

317
00:13:28,900 --> 00:13:30,320
Afraid of people laughing.

318
00:13:31,290 --> 00:13:34,120
Waiting was a way to hide from the fear.

319
00:13:34,180 --> 00:13:37,100
As long as I was "getting ready," I could not fail.

320
00:13:38,140 --> 00:13:39,580
But I could not start, either.

321
00:13:40,500 --> 00:13:43,700
The perfect moment was a wall I built to protect myself.

322
00:13:44,460 --> 00:13:46,520
A wall we build to protect ourselves.

323
00:13:47,020 --> 00:13:47,580
Yes, Ben.

324
00:13:48,220 --> 00:13:50,260
And behind the wall, it feels safe.

325
00:13:51,120 --> 00:13:54,740
Behind the wall, I am a person who will swim one day.

326
00:13:54,750 --> 00:13:55,730
A future swimmer.

327
00:13:56,420 --> 00:13:58,160
That is comfortable.

328
00:13:58,200 --> 00:14:01,380
The moment I go into the pool, I am a bad swimmer.

329
00:14:02,140 --> 00:14:03,860
A real, bad swimmer.

330
00:14:04,740 --> 00:14:06,520
A real, bad swimmer, Mia.

331
00:14:07,360 --> 00:14:08,500
That is not comfortable.

332
00:14:09,400 --> 00:14:10,140
But it is real.

333
00:14:11,120 --> 00:14:14,620
And a real bad swimmer becomes a real okay swimmer.

334
00:14:15,600 --> 00:14:17,520
A future swimmer becomes nothing.

335
00:14:18,560 --> 00:14:20,340
Future is where nothing happens.

336
00:14:21,160 --> 00:14:26,100
"Future is where nothing happens." Ben, that is the big idea of today.

337
00:14:26,780 --> 00:14:28,700
The perfect moment does not exist.

338
00:14:29,360 --> 00:14:30,700
What exists is now.

339
00:14:31,380 --> 00:14:34,460
And now is the only moment where anything ever started.

340
00:14:35,340 --> 00:14:36,860
Okay, so that was part two.

341
00:14:37,640 --> 00:14:39,460
Why the perfect moment never comes.

342
00:14:40,320 --> 00:14:42,940
We talked about the iPhone that was not finished.

343
00:14:42,980 --> 00:14:44,800
We said ready comes after the start.

344
00:14:45,440 --> 00:14:46,849
And we said waiting is a wall.

345
00:14:47,700 --> 00:14:49,399
Now, part three.

346
00:14:49,460 --> 00:14:50,960
Simple ideas for starting now.

347
00:14:51,620 --> 00:14:52,700
Ben, you go first.

348
00:14:53,500 --> 00:14:54,629
Okay, part three.

349
00:14:55,680 --> 00:14:57,600
My first idea is very simple, Mia.

350
00:14:58,420 --> 00:14:59,660
Make the start tiny.

351
00:15:00,600 --> 00:15:01,180
Two minutes.

352
00:15:01,980 --> 00:15:02,540
Ten minutes.

353
00:15:03,580 --> 00:15:05,700
One episode with a phone.

354
00:15:05,760 --> 00:15:07,460
Not the whole thing.

355
00:15:07,500 --> 00:15:09,180
The smallest piece of the thing.

356
00:15:10,120 --> 00:15:13,580
When the start is tiny, you do not need a perfect moment.

357
00:15:14,480 --> 00:15:16,820
Any moment is big enough for two minutes.

358
00:15:18,560 --> 00:15:20,640
Any moment is big enough for two minutes.

359
00:15:21,280 --> 00:15:22,020
I like that, Ben.

360
00:15:23,000 --> 00:15:25,280
My first idea is a strange one.

361
00:15:25,340 --> 00:15:27,700
Choose a bad moment on purpose.

362
00:15:27,760 --> 00:15:29,120
A rainy Tuesday.

363
00:15:29,160 --> 00:15:30,280
A tired evening.

364
00:15:30,880 --> 00:15:34,280
Because if I start on a bad moment, every other moment is better.

365
00:15:35,200 --> 00:15:37,220
My bad Tuesday was my swimming start.

366
00:15:37,820 --> 00:15:39,940
Now every swim is easier than that one.

367
00:15:40,760 --> 00:15:42,880
Every swim is easier than the first, Mia.

368
00:15:43,540 --> 00:15:44,420
That is smart.

369
00:15:45,120 --> 00:15:49,230
And I have a trick for the evening, because evenings are where I lose my starts.

370
00:15:50,060 --> 00:16:01,280
In the morning, I say, "Tonight, I will start." Then evening comes, and I am tired, and I say, "Tomorrow." So now I start in the morning, for two minutes.

371
00:16:02,320 --> 00:16:03,060
I open the file.

372
00:16:03,480 --> 00:16:05,200
I write one line.

373
00:16:05,240 --> 00:16:07,420
And in the evening, the thing is already started.

374
00:16:08,290 --> 00:16:12,200
"Start with energy, continue without it." That is smart, Ben.

375
00:16:12,760 --> 00:16:13,880
Continuing is easy.

376
00:16:14,380 --> 00:16:15,520
Starting is hard.

377
00:16:16,220 --> 00:16:20,020
So you start when you have energy, and you continue when you do not.

378
00:16:20,160 --> 00:16:21,660
I want to try that with my messages.

379
00:16:22,260 --> 00:16:23,440
Two lines in the morning.

380
00:16:23,980 --> 00:16:26,880
Then the message is started, and I only need to finish it.

381
00:16:28,580 --> 00:16:30,420
Choose a bad moment on purpose.

382
00:16:30,900 --> 00:16:32,120
That is brave, Mia.

383
00:16:33,020 --> 00:16:34,720
My second idea is about things.

384
00:16:35,420 --> 00:16:37,560
Make the thing ready, not yourself.

385
00:16:38,460 --> 00:16:42,000
We spend years making ourselves ready, and we are never ready.

386
00:16:42,780 --> 00:16:44,040
But a swimsuit can be ready.

387
00:16:44,640 --> 00:16:45,780
A phone can be ready.

388
00:16:46,360 --> 00:16:47,820
An open file can be ready.

389
00:16:48,620 --> 00:16:49,540
Things can be ready.

390
00:16:50,080 --> 00:16:50,820
People start.

391
00:16:51,690 --> 00:16:52,590
"Things can be ready.

392
00:16:52,950 --> 00:16:56,590
People start." Ben, that is exactly what I did.

393
00:16:57,200 --> 00:17:00,260
When I wanted to swim, I put my swimsuit in my work bag.

394
00:17:00,800 --> 00:17:02,150
Every day.

395
00:17:02,160 --> 00:17:06,080
So on the bad Tuesday, when I passed the pool, the swimsuit was there.

396
00:17:06,780 --> 00:17:08,220
I had no reason to wait.

397
00:17:08,780 --> 00:17:12,360
The perfect moment did not come, but the swimsuit was ready.

398
00:17:13,300 --> 00:17:14,960
The swimsuit was ready.

399
00:17:15,040 --> 00:17:15,980
That was enough, Mia.

400
00:17:16,830 --> 00:17:18,740
And my third idea comes from my sister.

401
00:17:19,540 --> 00:17:21,220
Tell one person.

402
00:17:21,300 --> 00:17:22,220
A sister.

403
00:17:22,280 --> 00:17:22,750
A friend.

404
00:17:23,230 --> 00:17:23,860
A colleague.

405
00:17:24,700 --> 00:17:30,980
Say, "I am going to start this week." Because a person will push you when the moment will not.

406
00:17:31,040 --> 00:17:32,020
Do you have a third idea?

407
00:17:32,940 --> 00:17:33,580
I do, Ben.

408
00:17:34,100 --> 00:17:36,360
My third idea is a question for the voice.

409
00:17:37,200 --> 00:17:41,320
Tonight, when we want to start something, the voice will say, "Not yet.

410
00:17:41,740 --> 00:17:42,360
Not today.

411
00:17:42,800 --> 00:17:45,940
Not ready." And I have one answer for that voice.

412
00:17:46,580 --> 00:17:52,390
Ask it, "When?" Not "why." "When?" And the voice has no answer.

413
00:17:52,920 --> 00:17:54,020
It never has a date.

414
00:17:54,540 --> 00:17:59,800
It only has "later." And when we see that the voice has no date, we can start.

415
00:18:00,700 --> 00:18:02,000
The voice has no date.

416
00:18:02,720 --> 00:18:04,380
That is true, Mia.

417
00:18:04,480 --> 00:18:09,280
I asked my voice, "When will I be ready for the podcast?" And it said nothing.

418
00:18:10,020 --> 00:18:11,300
Three years of nothing.

419
00:18:12,000 --> 00:18:13,360
The voice is not a plan.

420
00:18:13,760 --> 00:18:14,459
It is a feeling.

421
00:18:15,100 --> 00:18:18,660
And a feeling with no date is just fear wearing a nice coat.

422
00:18:19,570 --> 00:18:24,920
"Fear wearing a nice coat." Ben, I will remember that.

423
00:18:24,940 --> 00:18:26,630
And I have one more small idea.

424
00:18:27,300 --> 00:18:28,480
Say a sentence out loud.

425
00:18:29,110 --> 00:18:33,230
"Now is not perfect, and now is enough." I say it in the kitchen.

426
00:18:33,600 --> 00:18:34,960
It sounds silly.

427
00:18:35,020 --> 00:18:35,500
It works.

428
00:18:37,260 --> 00:18:39,400
It sounds silly, and it works, Mia.

429
00:18:39,940 --> 00:18:42,500
Like the sentence before bed in our slow day episode.

430
00:18:43,160 --> 00:18:44,720
Can I add a small one for messages?

431
00:18:45,280 --> 00:18:48,040
When I think, "I should write to my friend," I write.

432
00:18:48,500 --> 00:18:48,960
Right then.

433
00:18:49,580 --> 00:18:50,180
Two lines.

434
00:18:50,660 --> 00:18:52,100
Not a beautiful long message.

435
00:18:52,580 --> 00:18:56,800
Because if I wait for the time to write a beautiful long message, I write nothing.

436
00:18:57,420 --> 00:18:58,060
For a year.

437
00:18:59,300 --> 00:19:02,020
Two lines now is better than a perfect letter never.

438
00:19:02,540 --> 00:19:03,100
Yes, Ben.

439
00:19:03,580 --> 00:19:05,100
I learned that from my grandmother's letter.

440
00:19:05,830 --> 00:19:07,340
The perfect letter never came.

441
00:19:08,120 --> 00:19:10,060
Two lines would have come.

442
00:19:10,160 --> 00:19:12,320
So now, two lines, today.

443
00:19:12,980 --> 00:19:13,620
Not perfect.

444
00:19:14,440 --> 00:19:14,740
Sent.

445
00:19:15,580 --> 00:19:22,240
"Now is not perfect, and now is enough." Mia, I want to give that sentence to our listeners who are learning English.

446
00:19:22,820 --> 00:19:24,840
Because many of you are waiting to speak.

447
00:19:25,520 --> 00:19:27,100
So here is a tiny start.

448
00:19:27,780 --> 00:19:30,060
Say one sentence out loud, right now.

449
00:19:30,660 --> 00:19:31,380
Wherever you are.

450
00:19:32,000 --> 00:19:32,640
In the kitchen.

451
00:19:33,060 --> 00:19:34,880
On the bus, quietly.

452
00:19:34,960 --> 00:19:37,050
Say, "Today I started."

453
00:19:38,150 --> 00:19:41,420
"Today I started." That is all, everyone.

454
00:19:42,140 --> 00:19:42,650
Not ready.

455
00:19:43,000 --> 00:19:44,020
Grammar not perfect.

456
00:19:44,400 --> 00:19:45,140
Accent your own.

457
00:19:45,980 --> 00:19:47,440
And you started.

458
00:19:47,500 --> 00:19:49,800
On a normal Tuesday, with a podcast.

459
00:19:50,510 --> 00:19:51,140
That counts.

460
00:19:52,280 --> 00:19:53,780
Ben, is there one last idea?

461
00:19:54,740 --> 00:19:55,860
One last idea, Mia.

462
00:19:56,420 --> 00:19:57,640
It is about being bad.

463
00:19:58,380 --> 00:19:59,699
Everyone is bad at first.

464
00:20:00,280 --> 00:20:01,220
You were bad at swimming.

465
00:20:01,840 --> 00:20:03,040
I was bad at podcasts.

466
00:20:03,660 --> 00:20:05,580
Steve Jobs had a phone that could crash.

467
00:20:06,320 --> 00:20:08,040
Being bad is not a sign to wait.

468
00:20:08,520 --> 00:20:10,780
Being bad is the first step of being good.

469
00:20:11,560 --> 00:20:13,400
There is no road from waiting to good.

470
00:20:13,970 --> 00:20:16,880
The only road goes through bad.

471
00:20:16,910 --> 00:20:21,600
"The only road goes through bad." Ben, that is our message today, I think.

472
00:20:22,440 --> 00:20:23,160
Do not wait.

473
00:20:23,640 --> 00:20:24,500
Start small.

474
00:20:24,580 --> 00:20:25,140
Start bad.

475
00:20:25,440 --> 00:20:25,970
Start now.

476
00:20:26,760 --> 00:20:28,180
It is a Tuesday somewhere.

477
00:20:28,200 --> 00:20:29,660
It is raining somewhere.

478
00:20:29,680 --> 00:20:31,120
Somebody is tired.

479
00:20:31,140 --> 00:20:32,940
And somebody is starting anyway.

480
00:20:33,740 --> 00:20:34,700
Be that somebody.

481
00:20:35,620 --> 00:20:36,520
Be that somebody.

482
00:20:37,420 --> 00:20:42,470
Mia, let's repeat today's important words, slowly, because our listeners want to remember them.

483
00:20:43,560 --> 00:20:44,639
Wait.

484
00:20:44,649 --> 00:20:47,720
To stay and do nothing until something happens.

485
00:20:47,900 --> 00:20:49,700
I waited two years.

486
00:20:49,709 --> 00:20:51,520
Stop waiting.

487
00:20:51,560 --> 00:20:52,000
Perfect.

488
00:20:52,550 --> 00:20:54,540
With no problems, no mistakes.

489
00:20:55,120 --> 00:20:56,120
The perfect moment.

490
00:20:57,060 --> 00:20:58,020
Nothing is perfect.

491
00:20:59,040 --> 00:21:00,020
Start.

492
00:21:00,080 --> 00:21:00,640
To begin.

493
00:21:01,460 --> 00:21:02,080
Start small.

494
00:21:02,700 --> 00:21:03,340
Start now.

495
00:21:04,180 --> 00:21:04,460
Now.

496
00:21:05,100 --> 00:21:05,740
This moment.

497
00:21:06,300 --> 00:21:06,840
Not later.

498
00:21:07,780 --> 00:21:08,720
Now is enough.

499
00:21:09,800 --> 00:21:10,040
Ready.

500
00:21:10,660 --> 00:21:11,200
Prepared.

501
00:21:12,100 --> 00:21:12,840
I am not ready.

502
00:21:13,480 --> 00:21:14,340
Nobody is ready.

503
00:21:15,280 --> 00:21:15,580
Wait.

504
00:21:16,200 --> 00:21:16,640
Perfect.

505
00:21:17,420 --> 00:21:17,760
Start.

506
00:21:18,520 --> 00:21:18,800
Now.

507
00:21:19,760 --> 00:21:20,060
Ready.

508
00:21:20,940 --> 00:21:21,580
Five words.

509
00:21:22,060 --> 00:21:23,400
And one big idea.

510
00:21:24,180 --> 00:21:25,860
Stop waiting for the perfect moment.

511
00:21:26,900 --> 00:21:29,100
Now, Ben, do you have a question for our listeners?

512
00:21:29,960 --> 00:21:30,480
Yes, Mia.

513
00:21:31,020 --> 00:21:32,940
Everyone listening, here is my question.

514
00:21:33,560 --> 00:21:34,760
What are you waiting to start?

515
00:21:35,400 --> 00:21:35,800
Swimming?

516
00:21:36,360 --> 00:21:36,760
A call?

517
00:21:37,540 --> 00:21:38,240
Speaking English?

518
00:21:38,920 --> 00:21:39,240
A letter?

519
00:21:40,040 --> 00:21:42,340
Write it in the comments in one short sentence.

520
00:21:42,890 --> 00:21:50,460
And then, if you want, add three more words: "I start today." For example, "I am waiting to call my brother.

521
00:21:51,080 --> 00:21:51,880
I start today."

522
00:21:52,639 --> 00:21:57,980
"I start today." Reading those sentences is the best part of our week.

523
00:21:58,480 --> 00:22:02,379
And if this episode helped you, please subscribe to Two Cups English.

524
00:22:02,699 --> 00:22:04,919
We make a new episode every few days.

525
00:22:05,480 --> 00:22:09,620
Slow English, simple words, and one true story each time.

526
00:22:10,239 --> 00:22:12,739
The full text of this episode is in the description.

527
00:22:13,319 --> 00:22:14,399
Read while you listen.

528
00:22:14,999 --> 00:22:16,159
Or read later.

529
00:22:16,999 --> 00:22:17,940
Actually, no.

530
00:22:18,499 --> 00:22:19,039
Not later.

531
00:22:19,700 --> 00:22:20,259
Read now.

532
00:22:21,459 --> 00:22:23,179
Thank you for spending this time with us.

533
00:22:23,940 --> 00:22:25,339
Maybe you are on a bus.

534
00:22:25,959 --> 00:22:26,859
Maybe in the kitchen.

535
00:22:27,779 --> 00:22:29,599
Maybe it is a bad Tuesday.

536
00:22:30,739 --> 00:22:30,979
Good.

537
00:22:31,540 --> 00:22:33,499
Bad Tuesdays are where things begin.

538
00:22:34,679 --> 00:22:36,319
Say one sentence out loud.

539
00:22:37,399 --> 00:22:38,839
Put the swimsuit in the bag.

540
00:22:39,899 --> 00:22:40,699
Call the person.

541
00:22:41,919 --> 00:22:45,179
Now is not perfect, and now is enough.

542
00:22:47,219 --> 00:22:47,899
This is Mia.

543
00:22:48,259 --> 00:22:49,179
And this is Ben.

544
00:22:49,899 --> 00:22:51,599
This was Two Cups English.

545
00:22:52,319 --> 00:22:53,499
Stop waiting.

546
00:22:53,959 --> 00:22:55,200
We will see you next time.

